{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon","id":411308,"title":"Is there a homebrew Roman pantheon?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-19T04:30:48-08:00","id":411308,"title":"Is there a homebrew Roman pantheon?","content":"I am planning to do a scion campaign which is situated in the 3rd century A.D. during the Military Anarchy period in the Roman Empire. Now what I am wondering is if there is a decent homebrew Roman pantheon out there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4638-altered_b","name":"Altered_B","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T08:31:06-08:00","id":411594,"title":"","content":"The Greeks are basically doing double duty. There are a very, <i>very</i> few major Roman Gods that are not also Greek... and they're not <i>that</i> major. Probably not Legend 12. Janus, Selvans, etc. The biggest difference is just the names, and a few Associated Powers. Mars, for example, would have Fertility and maybe Guardian, whereas Ares does not have either. Ceres probably has Justice, while Demeter does not. It's a very limited number of changes.<br /> <br /> I can point you to my Roman Pantheon-specific Purview, if you'd like to replace Arete with <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rLFF53CaSnRwC0dnLwDgfa_ojSnJkSxQVFepqsyUZSU/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Civitas</a>.<br /> <br /> There just isn't a point in splitting up Jupiter and Zeus when all their stories, personalities and traits are virtually identical.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T16:44:12-08:00","id":412206,"title":"","content":"There are serious RELIGIOUS differences between how the Romans and Greeks saw their Gods. But Scion isn't about religion - it's about Myth. And the myths of the Roman, as recorded most prominently by Virgil and Ovid were either cribbed from the Greeks or built directly on those - hence them being tied together.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T09:34:28-08:00","id":412877,"title":"","content":"Total agreement with glamourweaver on the fundamentals of that. <br /> <br /> There are some aspect differences from the manner of worship that could be reflected game-wise, like Telgar mentioned. Before the Mars/Ares conflation in mythology, Mars was more of a &quot;protector of the fields&quot;, while Ares was &quot;spoiled psychotic bad-boy&quot;. Hestia may have stepped down the point of being a footnote, but Vesta was VERY important and had a lot to do with civic life and responsibility as opposed to &quot;I'll just sit here by the hearth. Don't trouble yourselves about me...&quot; And so on and so on. <br /> <br /> It definitely argues that you might want to play with their Purviews a bit. (Again a nod to Telgar). Since many of the Greek gods get a scant few other their PSP, it's not much of a problem, especially if you want to ditch Arete for a more Roman-flavored PSP. (How that should work mechanically, I leave up to, well, the gods. Unless you want temporo-cultural Purview swapping and switching over/developing a new PSP for the same pantheon to be a part of your game. Maybe Zeus, just said one day, &quot;Okay boys and girls, this Arete shit has got to go. Time for some civic responsibility.&quot;<br /> <br /> To me, it always seemed like the Roman gods were a little like the Greek gods who had just grown up a bit...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T16:16:35-08:00","id":413340,"title":"","content":"I wonder if Ares as spoiled psychotic bad boy might not reflect Athenian influence on Greek literature. I recall that the hill of Ares was a place for legal proceedings and orations; which is why when John Milton wrote his defense of intellectual freedom, he titled the essay &quot;Areopagitica.&quot; (s best I recall. The course where I read &quot;Areopagitica&quot; and learned the backstory was a long time ago.) Portrayals of the Greco-Roman Gods really should take account of their Roman side, too.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T23:06:40-08:00","id":413839,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=413340#post413340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder if Ares as spoiled psychotic bad boy might not reflect Athenian influence on Greek literature.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely - what we generally label &quot;Greek Myth&quot; is unabashedly Athenian Myth. I have no doubt if we had more Peleponesian writing, we'd see versions of Ares much more like Mars, and versions of Athena relegated to a virgin craft goddess like Minerva.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that despite the reputation of a bloody coward that Athenian literature gave him, Ares is also one of the only male Olympians without a rape myth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T08:49:39-08:00","id":414046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=413340#post413340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder if Ares as spoiled psychotic bad boy might not reflect Athenian influence on Greek literature. I recall that the hill of Ares was a place for legal proceedings and orations; which is why when John Milton wrote his defense of intellectual freedom, he titled the essay &quot;Areopagitica.&quot; (s best I recall. The course where I read &quot;Areopagitica&quot; and learned the backstory was a long time ago.) Portrayals of the Greco-Roman Gods really should take account of their Roman side, too.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</div> </div> </div> </div>The Areopagus proper is IN Athens. As far as I can remember, it's not a term for &quot;any hill on which we do legal proceedings&quot; in other cities. Take that for what you will, but if Athenian myth is the source of Ares as bad boy, there's a cognitive disconnect there.<br /> <br /> Ares is DEFINTELY a bad boy in the Iliad, which is not Athenian and much more seminal as myths go for the Greeks, as, at it's core, it largely predates the whole Ionian vs. Dorian dichotomy. In the Iliad, Ares fights until Diomedes actually wounds him then runs home to mama howling and weeping. There's your definitely spoiled child following blood-lusting warrior type. Aphrodite does the same &quot;spoiled child&quot; thing, and her mythology per se is not defined as Athenian, as that was not a place where her cult was very strong. She had a lot of traction out in the isles. Which, like Athens were largely Ionian, so the same mytho-types largely predominated.<br /> <br /> One of the definitive war gods of Sparta, which dominates the Peloponnese was Artemis. She was a homicidal bloody bitch who demanded that boys be whipped to death in her temple. That's definitely not something that gets picked up in Scion. She was less warrior, more hunter-ish in the Iliad, more petty than spoiled. And secretly saving Iphigenia after demanding her sacrifice is a little quixotic and not at all like the Artemis of Sparta. Then you take Artemis at Ephesus where she's a mother-goddess/goddess of the animals and plants/agriculture type, not AT ALL like any other Artemis (more Demeter or like the Macedonian cult of Rhea than Homeric Artemis). Then you take Diana, who is a weird synthesis, a goddess of the wild to be respected, but still pretty bloody-handed when you look at her King of the Grove cult at Aricia, which is very much core to how she was perceived in Rome. <br /> <br /> There is a couple of ultimate questions important to Scion implicit in these discussions. <br /> <br /> How schizoid are the gods in general? Do you meet a very different Diana in Aricia than you meet at Ephesus than how she is walking and talking in Athens vs. her behavior at Sparta? Are they all the same person or different aspects of the same person? Does she have any choice in how she is acting or is she somehow compelled to take on the Artemis of &quot;around here&quot;? Or is it incentivized somehow to act in accordance with your myths when and where they seem to matter?<br /> <br /> Second, how much credence do you give to cultic practice when you are building the deity in question? How much do you build into the system? Which myths do you pick when you have so many to choose from and so many missing, lost, etc.? The obvious answer is &quot;not much from cultic practice&quot; and &quot;not much that vary off the mainstream of how the god in question is commonly depicted in the modern world&quot;. Otherwise the game becomes less approachable and, ultimately, affects its success in attracting and keeping players.<br /> <br /> But, thinking around corners is so much fun.....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T16:14:37-08:00","id":414565,"title":"","content":"Interesting about Ares...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=413839#post413839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's worth noting that despite the reputation of a bloody coward that Athenian literature gave him, Ares is also one of the only male Olympians without a rape myth.</div> </div> </div> </div>That particular gives me pause.<br /> <br /> Like other things about certain Greek deities (Jim Butcher's take on Hades for another example) I have to wonder how bad were those that were given such negative press. Hera, is a Goddess of Fidelity and was always given such a hard time.<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Don't talk about my mother. When she stood up to Dad and his philandering, everyone called her a bitch. When she was quiet about it, everyone called her a doormat. Well, I'm tired of it. No one makes fun of the Queen of Heaven. Are we clear?&quot;<br /> ~Lord Mavors (aka Mars/Ares, the &quot;soldier&quot; from &quot;Orphans of Chaos&quot; by Wright)</i><br /> paraphrased a bit.<br /> <br /> But then there wasn't much good press around when talking of the Greek deities. Or at least for every good there was the bad that came with it.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T17:30:11-08:00","id":414630,"title":"","content":"Hera was given a hard time? With what? She was respected, powerful, authoritative. She had it going on. The male gods got tons of &quot;good press&quot; for themselves, as we can tell by how many movies we make about them. Hades, admittedly, gets a bad rep for being the death-god, which modern, Abrahamic cultures associate with the devil. Which doesn't make sense, because he gets the <i>good</i> dead people too, but &quot;Heaven&quot; is equated to Olympus, so &quot;Hell&quot; must be the one underground, right?<br /> <br /> The male Olympians, and <i>man</i><i>y</i> other figures, are just doing what their cultures wanted them to: being &quot;manly&quot; men, having sex, proving their virility, treating women as property. That was expected and encouraged. We don't do that any more because it's a wee bit evil. By our standards, yes, virtually <i>all</i> ancient religions are pretty evil. But that comes from the differences you get when cultures are thousands of years apart from each other. They'd consider us just as morally bankrupt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T19:37:46-08:00","id":414757,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/articles/h/hera.html\" target=\"_blank\">Hera</a><br /> <i><br /> Writers represented Hera as constantly being jealous of Zeus's various amorous affairs. She punished her rivals and their children, among both goddesses and mortals, with implacable fury. She placed two serpents in the cradle of <a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/articles/h/heracles.html\" target=\"_blank\">Heracles</a>; she had <a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/articles/i/io.html\" target=\"_blank\">Io</a> guarded by a hundred-eyed giant; she drove the foster-parents of <a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/articles/d/dionysus.html\" target=\"_blank\">Dionysus</a> mad, and tried to prevent the birth of <a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/articles/a/apollo.html\" target=\"_blank\">Apollo</a> and <a href=\"http://www.pantheon.org/articles/a/artemis.html\" target=\"_blank\">Artemis</a>. Even Zeus usually could not stand up to her. Sometimes when he got angry, he chained her to the mountain of Olympus by fastening anvils to her feet. However, most of the time Zeus resorted to stratagems: he either hid his illegitimate children, or he changed them into animals. </i><br /> -----","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T23:00:16-08:00","id":414941,"title":"","content":"It seems she's getting things taken care of. Her husband does things she does not approve of, she smites people. She isn't allowed to smite him, so she takes it out on the people she IS allowed to smite. And, on occasion, she does make it quite clear that he needs to cut that out if he wants to continue as her husband and the ruler of the Gods. She's doing pretty well for herself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T07:29:34-08:00","id":415109,"title":"","content":"It seems the goggles are rose-colored for some...For others, the language is pretty plain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T09:19:26-08:00","id":415263,"title":"","content":"So Encyclopedia Mythica is giving Hera a hard time? If not them, then to whom are you referring? <br /> <br /> Telgar is right. There is a lot of cultural relativism in dissing Hera. To the Greeks, particularly the Athenians, who weren't really a part of her fan club (Hera's cult wasn't particularly powerful in Athens) Hera was the epitome of the powerful wife. Sure, she couldn't forbid her husband to do all sorts of things that we think are super-bad, like adultery, but she was demonstrably powerful in that she could make infidelity a pain for his female partners. That's being a goddess of fidelity. As far as things Greek and Roman go, both parties are equally culpable and deserving of punishment. (And she DID work in punishments for him when and where she could) The fact that a lot of Zeus's paramours didn't get a say wasn't relevant. There are Ancient Greek laws where both the rapist and his victim were put to death. Horrible, awful, bad.... different. Taking on the children? Well, that's only smart. She didn't want any competition for her own children as his heirs. Dead, they can not coddle up to daddy, sit on his lap and get him to promise things, like, say, the Throne of Olympus if his butt should no longer occupy it. (And Artemis did get some pretty powerful promises from just such a cuddle session.) It's not like the lives of children are all that important anyway in the grand scheme of things. Certainly not as important as an adult's life. Horrible, awful, bad.... different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T10:26:48-08:00","id":415356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=415263#post415263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Encyclopedia Mythica is giving Hera a hard time? If not them, then to whom are you referring?<br /> <br /> Telgar is right. There is a lot of cultural relativism in dissing Hera. To the Greeks, particularly the Athenians, who weren't really a part of her fan club (Hera's cult wasn't particularly powerful in Athens) Hera was the epitome of the powerful wife. Sure, she couldn't forbid her husband to do all sorts of things that we think are super-bad, like adultery, but she was demonstrably powerful in that she could make infidelity a pain for his female partners. That's being a goddess of fidelity. As far as things Greek and Roman go, both parties are equally culpable and deserving of punishment. (And she DID work in punishments for him when and where she could) The fact that a lot of Zeus's paramours didn't get a say wasn't relevant. There are Ancient Greek laws where both the rapist and his victim were put to death. Horrible, awful, bad.... different. Taking on the children? Well, that's only smart. She didn't want any competition for her own children as his heirs. Dead, they can not coddle up to daddy, sit on his lap and get him to promise things, like, say, the Throne of Olympus if his butt should no longer occupy it. (And Artemis did get some pretty powerful promises from just such a cuddle session.) It's not like the lives of children are all that important anyway in the grand scheme of things. Certainly not as important as an adult's life. Horrible, awful, bad.... different.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your own words seem pretty clear too.<br /> <br /> Perhaps said writers were inspired by the Muses...o wait, aren't they the children of Zeus and Mnemosyne? I should wonder if they had a lot of nice things to say about Hera. Such as those things you are mentioning...<br /> <br /> Objectivity can be such a pain when writers aren't.<br /> <br /> But we play the game as we want. But if I were to look for a Greek deity that understood morality as it's known here and now, I wouldn't look any farther than Hera. If I had to look for a deity that understands the concept of &quot;duty&quot; as I understand it myself, I wouldn't look any farther than Hades.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T11:28:21-08:00","id":415461,"title":"","content":"I'm not even sure what you're saying anymore, Shadowflame. You started off saying that Hera was being given a hard time. By the standards of her culture, she was doing pretty well for herself. Even by the standards of our culture, she was respected, powerful and getting things done. Evil, yes, but still capable. The thing is that <i>all</i> the Greek gods are basically evil by our cultural standards. Yes, Hades too. Certainly Hera.<br /> <br /> What are you trying to articulate about the Greek gods? I'm not getting it. If it's that they're good people, I disagree. If it's that Hades is a respectable figure, I disagree (he kidnapped and raped his niece). If it's that Hera is a moral person, I disagree. Is it that Hera is weak or maligned by her myths? I disagree. Other than that, I've completely lost track of what you're trying to say.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T16:48:46-08:00","id":415833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=415461#post415461\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not even sure what you're saying anymore, Shadowflame.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not surprising that you lost track. If you keep in mind what I first posted then it gels. <i>And that's the writers of antiquity are suspect. </i><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=415461#post415461\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">.What are you trying to articulate about the Greek gods? I'm not getting it. If it's that they're good people, I disagree. If it's that Hades is a respectable figure, I disagree (he kidnapped and raped his niece). If it's that Hera is a moral person, I disagree. Is it that Hera is weak or maligned by her myths? I disagree.</div> </div> </div> </div>If that works for you...game on.<br /> But I'm going to see the likes of Ares, Hades, and yes, Hera as well, in a better light.<br /> <br /> If you wish to take issue with that, then I suggest you remember I'm one that favors putting scions of certain titans in the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T16:51:29-08:00","id":415836,"title":"","content":"Jim Butcher's portrayal of Hades may be a step away from portraying him as the Devil analogue, but it's definitely a selective viewpoint he took for the purposes of his fiction. (Including adding the whole &quot;keeper of the super-vault&quot; aspect from Pluto, which is actually pertinent to this thread!). <br /> <br /> Hades wasn't any more or less of a stand-up guy and a model of dutiful behavior than most of the other Greek gods. In addition to kidnapping and raping his niece, he them imprisons her, and arranges for her to be tricked so that becomes a standing biannual thing. There's a lot more things Hades does that aren't the &quot;model of duty&quot; in modern terms... setting up the whole &quot;Gotcha&quot; on Orpheus with the Eurydice thing for shits and grins (guess he really didn't like the music all that much, huh?), etc. etc. <br /> <br /> He is also not dutiful according to the terms of the Ancient Greeks. He likes to trick heroes into sitting down at his dinner table then pins them in place Siege Perilous style. That's a complete abrogation of his duties as host to his guests and that is a VERY BAD THING to the Greeks. <br /> <br /> I would look elsewhere for a model of duty among the Greek gods. How about Hygenia? She gives her job up to her daddy's boyfriend so daddy can keep him nearby. She marries Heracles when commanded to do so. She's the daughter of Zeus and Hera, so she's pretty much a Princess of Heaven, and she never ever ever seems to do anything bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T17:13:15-08:00","id":415892,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=415836#post415836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Jim Butcher's portrayal of Hades may be a step away from portraying him as the Devil analogue, but it's definitely a selective viewpoint he took for the purposes of his fiction.</div> </div> </div> </div>And it works for me.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T21:12:37-08:00","id":416122,"title":"","content":"I wasn't talking about the game, Shadowflame. I was talking about how these deities are portrayed <i>in their own mythology</i>. How can the writers of antiquity be suspect when they're the primary source? You can't challenge a primary source. <i>It's the primary source</i>. That's like saying you disagree with Harry Potter's father being James Potter. It makes no sense, because the primary source (Harry Potter books) specifically says that James Potter <i>is</i> Harry Potter's father and offers no suggestion otherwise. <br /> <br /> You're certainly free to <i>portray</i> the Greek gods in a &quot;better light.&quot; Any author re-purposing them for modern fiction is, as Jim Butcher and Disney animators did. That's nothing new. You did not make it clear you were changing from &quot;talking about mythology&quot; to &quot;talking about how I want to portray things in my own settings.&quot; That's a pretty important divergence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-23T04:45:08-08:00","id":416382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=415836#post415836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">He is also not dutiful according to the terms of the Ancient Greeks. He likes to trick heroes into sitting down at his dinner table then pins them in place Siege Perilous style. That's a complete abrogation of his duties as host to his guests and that is a VERY BAD THING to the Greeks.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theseus#The_abduction_of_Persephone_and_encounter_with_Hades\" target=\"_blank\">Theseus and his friend were attempting to abduct Persephone</a>, which is an even more severe violation of hospitality. I think Hades can probably not be judged too harshly for that particular offense, his reaction was a lot milder than response of virtually all of Greece to the similar abduction of Helen.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not the <a href=\"https://www.theoi.com/Daimon/Alastor.html\" target=\"_blank\">daimon</a>, just a namesake.","edited":{"userid":858,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/858-alastor","user":"Alastor","date":". 10-31-2020, 10:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/858-alastor","name":"Alastor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-23T04:46:38-08:00","id":416383,"title":"","content":"I like to make use of the different interpretation of Gods as them changing over time, it makes them more human rather than weird abstract personifications","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-23T07:04:33-08:00","id":416473,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=416383#post416383\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like to make use of the different interpretation of Gods as them changing over time, it makes them more human rather than weird abstract personifications</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be a shame to keep them frozen in time. Unlike the society that they came from, which has grown and matured. Ares, in the book I quoted above, has did just that; he went from being a god of war for its own sake to a war weary general who states in that book that there are no winners in a war. If nothing else, it certainly shows less the Greek &quot;Ares&quot; and more the Roman &quot;Mars&quot; which came later.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-23T08:53:03-08:00","id":416527,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=415892#post415892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And it works for me.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Working for you&quot; and having something to do with the sources are completely different things. You started this off by saying Hera/Juno among others (Ares/Mars specifically, I guess) received bad press and that it wasn't warranted. Then you use said modern sources and a pretty short article from a generalized online dictionary as your sources. Then counter any arguments based on actual mythology with personal preference not with the mythology in question. You can't argue that modern fantasy reinterpretations of Greek or Roman gods (what Jim Butcher has to say, or the depictions of Greek gods in other books, movies, TV Shows, video games) are as fundamental as what Homer and Ovid have to say. You can certainly agree with the modern guys and use them as you will in your own games, and it's a lot of fun to see how other people use the same material, but Jim Butcher's Hades is not the Hades of the myths. He's a modern reinterpretation.You can't argue that Hades is the greatest guy among all the gods because Jim Butcher decided to ignore that he abducted his future wife, raped her, imprisoned her and then insured that she was stuck with the deal. I could counter your argument on Hades, saying that he was a total dick based on the Percy Jackson movie. One could very legitimately argue that what Butcher did was pretty odious. He deliberately brushed any bad aspects of Hades under the table and even did so with a sort of hand-waving, &quot;Don't believe the bad press.&quot; And, yup, those are modern sensibilities not Ancient Greek. It's about my feelings on the whole thing, my modern feelings from a modern perspective, identified as such and I'm making certain to distinguish it from the way Hades is portrayed in myths.<br /> <br /> <br /> Depict Hera how you want to for your games. That's totally cool. Same with Hades. And everyone else from Greek and other Pantheons. My take on Hera and Hades is certainly that of gods that have changed with the times. Hera has manipulated human society to create the concept of no-fault divorce, moved to CA and divorced Zeus. She's asking for half of Olympus as a part of the settlement. It's definitely a different take on fidelity and marriage. A very modern one. It's divided the pantheon right when they have other things to do (e.g. the new Titanomachy). Some of the divisions have to do with the nature of the god in question. E.g. Athena helped set up those laws so she feels obligated to support them, even though she isn't a big fan of Hera or she of her. Some of the divisions have more to do with the interpersonal relationships... Hephaestus has sided with Zeus, because Aphrodite and Ares sided with Hera. Etc. Etc. My take on Hades is that he's a cold-eyed killer and a fucking bastard who is 100% loyal and dutiful, but not in a nice way at all. He was OWED a wife, so, when he didn't get one, he TOOK one. That's my take on the Persephone myth, based on the myths, but definitely skewed to the way I wanted Hades to come across. In one of my Scion games, he stole another Scion's child posthumously, resurrected him and became his adoptive father, with little choice for the poor PC... &quot;I'm your dad, or you can stay dead.&quot; That is definitely not Jim Butcher's Hades, nor is it Rick Riordan's. It's all mine. I like it. But I'm not going to say that it's the definitive modern Hades. Of course, I think mine is better, but I should think that. If not, I would probably use Butcher or Riordan or someone else's. Probably not Butcher's. That part of the book rang very false for me. I like his Odin much better. Still wouldn't use it, but don't dislike it.<br /> <br /> In retrospect, I missed an opportunity using just the Greek versions of the gods for those two characterizations. They would be even more robust if I had calculated in the Roman slant. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":".  Bringing it back to the OP 02-23-2015, 12:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-23T08:58:56-08:00","id":416528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Alastor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/411308-is-there-a-homebrew-roman-pantheon?p=416382#post416382\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theseus#The_abduction_of_Persephone_and_encounter_with_Hades\" target=\"_blank\">Theseus and his friend were attempting to abduct Persephone</a>, which is an even more severe violation of hospitality. I think Hades can probably not be judged to harshly for that particular offense, his reaction was a lot milder than response of virtually all of Greece to the similar abduction of Helen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. Hades appears to be violating xenia. <br /> <br /> I think I can play lawyer with it and say that Theseus, in coming to Hades specifically to kidnap his wife violated it first. Then you have to decide if Hades should have to keep up his part or if Theseus's plans meant Hades didn't have to hold to his role. I'd say it is a continuation of his dick-ish-ness in the whole Persephone situation. He could have just told Hecate to turn them around at the crossroads and sent them right back to the lands of the living if he hadn't want to emphatically point out that Persephone was HIS (at least during those 6 months). But that's just a take on the situation based on what you pointed out. And me changing my original answer. A total lawyer thing to do. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}